 CO4.2  BUFFER CACHE HIT RATIO (LRU, delayed write)
==============================================================================
   Trace: 20,000 operations (16,385 reads / 3,615 writes)
   Working sets: metadata 32 blocks, directories 128 blocks,
                 log 241 blocks, VM streaming 59,000 blocks

     Cache size   Cache MB      Hits    Misses   Hit ratio   Disk writes
             16       0.06     1,721    18,279      8.61%         3,564
             32       0.12     3,256    16,744     16.28%         3,516
             64       0.25     5,730    14,270     28.65%         3,414
            128       0.50     8,708    11,292     43.54%         3,188
            256       1.00    11,310     8,690     56.55%         2,586
            512       2.00    13,696     6,304     68.48%         1,583
           1024       4.00    15,389     4,611     76.94%           469
           2048       8.00    15,671     4,329     78.35%           243

   Reading of the curve: 80% of operations touch a hot set of only
   401 blocks (metadata + directories + the log tail), and 20% stream
   the 59,000-block VM image region, which no realistic cache can hold.
   The achievable ceiling is therefore about 80%, and 1,024 buffers
   (4 MB) already reach 76.9% -- 96% of everything available. Doubling
   the cache again buys 1.4 more percentage points for another 4 MB.
   The right fix is not a bigger cache but O_DIRECT on VM image I/O,
   so streaming traffic stops evicting the metadata that caches well.

   Note the write column: delayed write means a block modified many
   times reaches the platter once, but any block still dirty at a
   crash is lost. That is the reliability/performance trade-off that
   fsync() and the journal exist to arbitrate.

